An ablation study variant of differentiable volume rendering optimization that replaces Gaussian noise assumptions with a positive Student's t-distribution to model gradient uncertainty. It serves as a controlled baseline to isolate the impact of heavy-tailed noise modeling from other components like MCMC sampling, ensuring fair comparison with methods using low-pass filtering. It builds on the Gradient Descent Solver [3280-gradient-descent-solver.md] by modifying the noise model. Inputs and Outputs: Not given in the sources. Data Structures: Not given in the sources. Steps: 1. Replace Gaussian noise sampling with sampling from a Student's t-distribution. 2. Remove negative components from the sampled noise or update. 3. Remove SGHMC (Stochastic Gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo) dynamics. 4. Apply a low-pass filter with a 0.3 diagonal offset to the update or parameters. Formulas: The probability density function for a Student's t-distribution is: f(t | 
