u is degrees of freedom. The "positive" constraint implies restricting the support or sampling mechanism to non-negative values for the noise component. Parameters: 
u (degrees of freedom): Controls tail heaviness. Not given in the sources. \alpha (learning rate): Standard SGD learning rate. Not given in the sources. Low-pass filter offset: 0.3 diagonal offset. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def sgdpositivetupdate(theta, gradient, alpha, nu, scale): # Sample from Student's t-distribution with nu degrees of freedom and scale z = standardnormal() v = chisquared(nu) noise = z scale / sqrt(v / nu) # Remove negative components as per method definition noise = max(noise, 0.0) # Combine gradient and noise update = gradient + noise # Apply low-pass filter with 0.3 diagonal offset # Exact application of the diagonal offset is not specified in sources # Apply learning rate and update parameters thetanew = theta - alpha update return thetanew Edge Cases: Not given in the sources. An optimization technique to find altered scattering parameters (g, σₛ) that minimize an error function d(g, σₛ) between a rendered image and a target image, using a reparameterized search space to avoid local minima. Performance: Used in ablation studies to compare against methods with similar component counts, showing competitive LPIPS scores on Mip-NeRF 360 and Tanks&Temples datasets. Strengths: Provides a robust baseline for heavy-tailed noise modeling; fair comparison with filtered methods. Not given in the sources. 3D Student Splatting and Scooping, https://doi.org/10.1109/cvpr52734.2025.01960, fetched 2026-10-29

Gradient scaling is a technique for differentiable volume rendering optimization that prevents background collapse and near-camera floaters by counter-balancing the sampling density imbalance close to cameras. It sets itself apart from alternatives like near-plane clipping or occlusion losses by addressing the root cause (gradient magnitude imbalance) directly during backpropagation, rather than imposing priors on density distribution or restricting ray origins. Inputs and Outputs: The method takes per-point gradients for color (
