abla p} = \delta{ip}^2) without the \min(1, \dots) clamp leads to very strong gradients far from the camera, preventing correct surface learning and causing a bias toward far density. Near Plane Zero: Setting the near plane to zero allocates slightly fewer samples to the center of the scene, which can have a slightly negative impact on reconstruction when there is no near-camera geometry. Scene Scale: If the scene scale is significantly different from 1 unit, the default formula fails and \sigma must be adjusted based on camera calibration. The specific variant using \min(1, \delta{ip}^2). It is chosen over pure quadratic scaling to prevent far-density bias. See Simple-RF. Using \sigma to adjust for non-unit scenes. Chosen when camera calibration data is available and scene bounds are known. See Simple-RF. Time and Memory Complexity: The operation induces an overhead of 100µs in the backward pass for 300k points, which is negligible compared to ~50ms iteration times. Measured Performance: DVGOv2, TensoRF, Instant NGP: Drastically reduces near-camera floaters and prevents density buildup near cameras, leading to more coherent depth and better geometry reconstruction compared to "No Scaling". MipNeRF360 'Stump': With L{dist}, near plane np=0, and scaling s{
abla p}, the PSNR is 26.62. Comparison to Alternatives: vs. No Scaling: Reduces floaters and prevents background collapse. vs. Quadratic Scaling (uncapped): Correctly reconstructs geometry early and prevents near-camera density buildup, whereas uncapped scaling leads to bad reconstruction of far density. vs. L{dist} Loss: L{dist} forces density to concentrate around a single point, which can harm semi-transparent surfaces, while gradient scaling does not impose such priors. vs. FreeNeRF Occlusion Loss: FreeNeRF penalizes density near the camera, imposing a prior, while gradient scaling addresses the root cause (gradient imbalance) without imposing density priors. Strengths and Limitations: Strengths: Compatible with most NeRF implementations (MLP, voxel hash-grid, tensor decomposition, plain voxels); removes the need for near planes; lightweight implementation; improves visual quality and quantitative metrics (PSNR, SSIM). Limitations: Quantitative metrics like PSNR may not fully reflect visual quality improvements if floaters are only visible in sparse test views. Setting near plane to zero can slightly reduce sample density in the scene center. Origin: The method was proposed by J. Philip and V. Deschaintre in the paper "Floaters No More: Radiance Field Gradient Scaling for Improved Near-Camera Training". History and Development: NeRF-based methods typically require careful choice of near planes to avoid background collapse, where density builds up close to the cameras, creating floating artifacts. The authors identified that this is caused by a higher density of samples in regions near cameras due to the 1/\delta{ip}^2 visibility decay, leading to disproportionate gradients. The method was demonstrated on DVGOv2, TensoRF, Instant NGP, and MipNeRF 360. Usage: It is used in various neural radiance field optimization pipelines to improve training stability and visual quality, particularly in unbounded scenes or when near-plane constraints are undesirable. Floaters No More: Radiance Field Gradient Scaling for Improved Near-Camera Training, https://doi.org/10.2312/sr.20231122, fetched 2026-10-29

This method determines the entry and exit points of a ray intersecting a tetrahedron by representing the ray and tetrahedron edges as Plücker coordinates, computing permuted inner products to classify faces, and deriving scaled barycentric coordinates directly from these products. It sets itself apart from plane-intersection approaches by avoiding explicit plane calculations and divisions until the final normalization step, and from ray-triangle decomposition methods (like Moller-Trumbore) by leveraging shared edge computations and early termination logic specific to the tetrahedron's topology. Inputs and Outputs Input: Ray defined by origin \mathbf{P} and direction \mathbf{L} (infinite directed line); Tetrahedron defined by vertices \mathbf{V}0, \mathbf{V}1, \mathbf{V}2, \mathbf{V}3. Output: Entry face F{enter}, exit face F{leave}, entry point \mathbf{P}{enter}, exit point \mathbf{P}{leave}, and their respective barycentric coordinates. Data Structures Tetrahedron Vertices: \mathbf{V}0, \mathbf{V}1, \mathbf{V}2, \mathbf{V}3. Faces: Indexed by the opposite vertex. F3: Vertices \mathbf{V}0, \mathbf{V}1, \mathbf{V}2. F2: Vertices \mathbf{V}1, \mathbf{V}0, \mathbf{V}3. F1: Vertices \mathbf{V}2, \mathbf{V}3, \mathbf{V}0. F0: Vertices \mathbf{V}3, \mathbf{V}2, \mathbf{V}1. Face Vertices: For face Fi, vertices are denoted \mathbf{V}{i0}, \mathbf{V}{i1}, \mathbf{V}{i2}. Edges: Edges of face Fi are subscripted by the index of the opposite vertex. e{i0}: Edge \mathbf{V}{i1}\mathbf{V}{i2}. e{i1}: Edge \mathbf{V}{i2}\mathbf{V}{i0}. e{i2}: Edge \mathbf{V}{i0}\mathbf{V}{i1}. Orientation: The tetrahedron is assumed oriented such that the outward-pointing normal of face Fi is directed away from vertex \mathbf{V}i. Plücker Coordinates A directed line is represented by a six-vector  oldsymbol{\pi} = \{\mathbf{p}: \mathbf{q}\}, where \mathbf{p} is the direction vector and \mathbf{q} = \mathbf{p} 	imes \mathbf{o} is the moment vector, with \mathbf{o} being any point on the line. Ray Plücker:  oldsymbol{\pi}r = \{\mathbf{L}: \mathbf{L} 	imes \mathbf{P}\} = \{\mathbf{p}r: \mathbf{q}r\}. Edge Plücker: For a directed line from \mathbf{A} to \mathbf{B},  oldsymbol{\pi}{AB} = \{\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{A}: (\mathbf{B}-\mathbf{A}) 	imes \mathbf{A}\}. Permuted Inner Product The relative orientation of two lines r and s is determined by the permuted inner product:  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}s = \mathbf{p}r \cdot \mathbf{q}s + \mathbf{q}r \cdot \mathbf{p}s If  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}s > 0, line s passes counterclockwise around line r. If  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}s < 0, line s passes clockwise around line r. If  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}s = 0, lines intersect or are parallel. Note:  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}{AB} = -( oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}{BA}). Algorithm Steps 1. Initialize: Set F{enter} = 	ext{nil}, F{leave} = 	ext{nil}. 2. Loop Faces: Iterate i from 3 down to 0 (faces F3, F2, F1, F0). 3. Compute Signs: For face Fi, compute signs \sigma{i0}, \sigma{i1}, \sigma{i2} for its three edges e{i0}, e{i1}, e{i2}: \sigma{ij} = 	ext{sign}( oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}{e{ij}}) Where 	ext{sign}(x) is 1 if x>0, 0 if x=0, and -1 if x<0. 4. Optimization (Early Exit in Face Test): Compute \sigma{i0} and \sigma{i1}. If \sigma{i0} 
eq \sigma{i1} and neither is zero, the face is not intersected (signs disagree). Skip computing \sigma{i2} and proceed to next face. Otherwise, compute \sigma{i2} if not already determined. 5. Determine Face Sign: If \sigma{i0} 
eq 0, let \sigmai = \sigma{i0}. Else if \sigma{i1} 
eq 0, let \sigmai = \sigma{i1}. Else \sigmai = \sigma{i2}. 6. Classify Face: If \sigmai 
eq 0 and (\sigma{i2} == \sigmai 	ext{ or } \sigma{i2} == 0): If \sigmai > 0, set F{enter} = Fi. If \sigmai < 0, set F{leave} = Fi. 7. Post-First-Face Optimization: If one face (e.g., F{enter}) is found, edges shared with it have conforming signs and need not be tested again. For remaining faces, if F{leave} is not found, all edge signs must satisfy \sigma{ij} \le 0. If F{enter} is not found, all edge signs must satisfy \sigma{ij} \ge 0. 8. Two Faces Left Optimization: If F{leave} must be determined between F1(\mathbf{V}2\mathbf{V}3\mathbf{V}0) and F0(\mathbf{V}3\mathbf{V}2\mathbf{V}1), check the common edge \mathbf{V}2\mathbf{V}3. If  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}{\mathbf{V}2\mathbf{V}3} < 0, then F{leave} = F1. Otherwise, F{leave} = F0 (unless all \sigma{0j} are zero, in which case F{leave} = F1). 9. Termination: If both F{enter} and F{leave} are determined, terminate. 10. Barycentric Coordinates: Compute scaled barycentric coordinates for the intersection points on the faces: wi =  oldsymbol{\pi}r \odot  oldsymbol{\pi}{ei} ui =  rac{wi}{\sum{k=0}^{2} wk} (Note: The sum is over the three edges of the specific face, not all four faces). Compute Cartesian coordinates of intersection point \mathbf{P}k: \mathbf{P}k = u0 \mathbf{V}0 + u1 \mathbf{V}1 + u2 \mathbf{V}2 Solve for parametric distance tk using a coordinate of \mathbf{P}k where \mathbf{L} is non-zero: \mathbf{P}k = \mathbf{P} + tk \mathbf{L} Pseudocode Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def pluckerraytetrahedronintersection(rayorigin, raydir, tetvertices): """ rayorigin: tuple (x, y, z) raydir: tuple (x, y, z) tetvertices: list of 4 tuples [(x0,y0,z0), (x1,y1,z1), (x2,y2,z2), (x3,y3,z3)] Returns: (Fenter, Fleave, Penter, Pleave) or None if no intersection """ pr = raydir qr = cross(raydir, rayorigin) Fenter = None Fleave = None # Face vertex mapping: Fi vertices are Vi0, Vi1, Vi2 facevertices = [ [tetvertices[0], tetvertices[1], tetvertices[2]], # F3 [tetvertices[1], tetvertices[0], tetvertices[3]], # F2 [tetvertices[2], tetvertices[3], tetvertices[0]], # F1 [tetvertices[3], tetvertices[2], tetvertices[1]] # F0 ] # Store scaled barycentric weights for each face to compute intersection points later faceweights = [None, None, None, None] for i in [3, 2, 1, 0]: if Fenter is not None and Fleave is not None: break verts = facevertices[i] v0, v1, v2 = verts[0], verts[1], verts[2] # Edge 0: v1 -> v2 dir0 = sub(v2, v1) q0 = cross(dir0, v1) pi0 = dot(pr, q0) + dot(qr, dir0) sigma0 = sign(pi0) # Edge 1: v2 -> v0 dir1 = sub(v0, v2) q1 = cross(dir1, v2) pi1 = dot(pr, q1) + dot(qr, dir1) sigma1 = sign(pi1) # Optimization: if signs disagree and neither is zero, face is not intersected if sigma0 != sigma1 and sigma0 != 0 and sigma1 != 0: continue # Edge 2: v0 -> v1 dir2 = sub(v1, v0) q2 = cross(dir2, v0) pi2 = dot(pr, q2) + dot(qr, dir2) sigma2 = sign(pi2) # Determine face sign sigmai if sigma0 != 0: sigmai = sigma0 elif sigma1 != 0: sigmai = sigma1 else: sigmai = sigma2 # Classify face if sigmai != 0 and (sigma2 == sigmai or sigma2 == 0): if sigmai > 0: Fenter = i else: Fleave = i # Store weights for barycentric calculation if face is entry or exit if Fenter == i or Fleave == i: faceweights[i] = [pi0, pi1, pi2] if Fenter is None or Fleave is None: return None # Compute intersection points and parametric distances def computeintersection(faceidx, weights): w0, w1, w2 = weights sumw = w0 + w1 + w2 if sumw == 0: return None, 0.0 # Degenerate u0, u1, u2 = w0/sumw, w1/sumw, w2/sumw verts = facevertices[faceidx] Pk = (u0verts[0][0] + u1verts[1][0] + u2verts[2][0], u0verts[0][1] + u1verts[1][1] + u2verts[2][1], u0verts[0][2] + u1verts[1][2] + u2verts[2][2]) # Solve Pk = P + tL for t using a non-zero component of L if raydir[0] != 0: t = (Pk[0] - rayorigin[0]) / raydir[0] elif raydir[1] != 0: t = (Pk[1] - rayorigin[1]) / raydir[1] else: t = (Pk[2] - rayorigin[2]) / raydir[2] return Pk, t Penter, tenter = computeintersection(Fenter, faceweights[Fenter]) Pleave, tleave = computeintersection(Fleave, faceweights[Fleave]) return (Fenter, Fleave, Penter, Pleave) def sign(x): return 1 if x > 0 else (-1 if x < 0 else 0) def sub(a, b): return (a[0]-b[0], a[1]-b[1], a[2]-b[2]) def cross(a, b): return (a[1]b[2]-a[2]b[1], a[2]b[0]-a[0]b[2], a[0]b[1]-a[1]b[0]) def dot(a, b): return a[0]b[0] + a[1]b[1] + a[2]b[2] Edge Cases and Pitfalls Sliding Interfaces: Interior faces must be shared by exactly two tetrahedra. Sliding interfaces (tetrahedra sharing only part of a face) are not allowed for the connectivity optimization to work. Numerical Ambiguity: Ambiguity at shared edges due to numerical issues can introduce holes or overlaps. An additional Plücker test can resolve this. Edge Exit: If the ray leaves the last tetrahedron through an edge, the next tetrahedron cannot be determined directly via connectivity. Zero Signs: Any two (but not all three) signs \sigma{ij} may be zero. The logic for determining the face sign \sigmai must handle cases where \sigma{i0} and \sigma{i1} are both zero, falling back to \sigma{i2}. Edge Orientation: The sign of the permuted inner product changes if the edge direction is reversed. This must be handled when reusing computations for shared edges. A traversal method for hexahedral meshes that uses Plücker coordinates to determine the exit face of a ray intersecting a hexahedral cell, requiring face decomposition into triangles for interpolation. It sets itself apart from its sibling Hexahedral Mesh Traversal (Bilinear Patch-Extension) by relying on triangular decomposition for interpolation, which introduces parametric discontinuities along the diagonal but allows unambiguous face determination. Time Complexity: The optimized algorithm reduces the average number of Plücker tests to 2.67 per tetrahedron, compared to 3-9 tests in the naive approach. Memory Complexity: Requires 16 additional bytes per tetrahedron for connectivity information. Pre-computing Plücker coordinates doubles memory requirements, so on-the-fly conversion is preferred. Performance: Achieves 15.5 million tetrahedra per second on a Dual-core Opteron with 2GHz. Represents a 55% average increase in raw performance compared to the naive approach. Strengths: Directly provides scaled barycentric coordinates from Plücker tests, avoiding division until the final normalization step. Outperforms Haines' algorithm and Moller/Trumbore-based algorithms in all tested cases, including those with precomputed quantities. Limitations: Cannot apply parametric distance restrictions (e.g., max ray length) early; these must be applied as post-processing. Haines' algorithm performs comparatively better when few ray-tetrahedron pairs intersect because it can sometimes decide there is no intersection without examining all four faces. When to Use: When high-performance ray-tetrahedron intersection is required, especially in the context of tetrahedral mesh traversal where connectivity can be exploited. When Not to Use: When the mesh has sliding interfaces or when early parametric distance culling is critical and the overhead of post-processing is unacceptable. The method was introduced by Platis and Theoharis in 2003. It builds on the properties of Plücker coordinates for representing directed lines in 3D space. The approach extends Garrity's grid-based traversal method [Gar90] and Wald's kd-tree implementation for initial tetrahedron search [Wal04]. It is used in ray tracing of triangular meshes (cited in [AC97] and [SF01] as equivalent 4x4 determinant method) and tetrahedral meshes. Fast Ray Traversal of Tetrahedral and Hexahedral Meshes for Direct Volume Rendering, https://doi.org/10.2312/vissym/eurovis06/235-242, fetched 2026-10-24 Interactive Volume Rendering Techniques, https://www.scribd.com/document/479714183/Interactive-Volume-Rendering-with-Ray-Tracing, fetched 2026-10-25 (unofficial) Fast Ray–Tetrahedron Intersection using Plücker..., 